WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.060
Good morning, Hank. It's Tuesday, July 30th, 2013 and I'm back!

00:00:03.139 --> 00:00:06.710
I missed you guys so much! But I've really had a wonderful paternity

00:00:06.710 --> 00:00:10.670
leave. It's just so great to be home with my
family and my beautiful baby dau-

00:00:10.670 --> 00:00:12.020
Am I...

00:00:12.020 --> 00:00:14.040
Am I in an airport?! Dang it!

00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:17.920
So, Hank, I'm on my way to VidCon...  *muffled airport announcement*

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:21.300
Oh, I can't smoke?
I can't smoke inside of a public building?

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:25.369
That's a shock! Can I snort heroin? Oh?
I'm not allowed to snort heroin either?

00:00:25.369 --> 00:00:29.490
What is this, an airport or a monastery? Right, but before I was in this airport on my

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:33.350
way to VidCon, I spent the last month
with my family on the United States'

00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:35.079
third coast: Lake Michigan.

00:00:35.079 --> 00:00:38.640
There was the beautiful water and
astonishing sunsets and sometimes people

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:41.600
would pretend to have a war to celebrate
America's independence.

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:44.450
There were also a lot of Nerdfighters there
on the coast of Lake Michigan, although I

00:00:44.450 --> 00:00:45.379
never met them

00:00:45.379 --> 00:00:49.789
until and unless I removed my shirt at
the beach, at which point inevitably

00:00:49.789 --> 00:00:53.053
someone would walk up to me and be like, "Are
you John Green?" And I would be like...

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:54.380
"*nervous mumble* yesss..."

00:00:54.520 --> 00:00:57.280
Right, I want to tell you about a funny thing that happened, but I have to go get on the plane

00:00:57.280 --> 00:00:59.219
so I'll tell you in California. MOVIE....

00:00:59.219 --> 00:01:02.699
MAGIC! That joke was six hours in the
making. So Hank, as a preface to the story

00:01:02.699 --> 00:01:05.880
you have to understand that while my son
Henry is very bright, he is also three

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:08.110
and a half years old. So he rarely says

00:01:08.110 --> 00:01:12.630
things that surprise me because, you know,
like, I taught him all the words he knows.

00:01:12.630 --> 00:01:15.280
Okay, so when we first get to Michigan
Henry and I go down to the beach and

00:01:15.290 --> 00:01:16.500
we're digging holes, you know,

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:19.770
looking for treasure. And he says, "Daddy, I
wanna dig a big hole! I want to dig

00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:23.390
all the way the tippy tippy bottom!" 
(which, of course, is the opposite of the tippy tippy top).

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:26.380
And I said, "Yeah! Let's keep digging!" and Henry said, "Daddy, this is

00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:31.180
so fun!" And I said, "I know!", and then he
said, "Daddy, do you know who likes digging holes?"

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:34.659
And I said, "No. Who?" and he looked at me and
he said, "Germans".

00:01:34.659 --> 00:01:38.369
Okay, so when Henry was one we lived for a
little while in the Netherlands while I

00:01:38.369 --> 00:01:41.220
was researching The Fault in Our Stars. And we kept
running into Dutch people

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:44.409
who would tell us that they didn't
really think that highly of Germans.

00:01:44.409 --> 00:01:48.009
And I would be like, "Oh, yeah, because of the
Second World War and the Occupation

00:01:48.009 --> 00:01:51.479
and everything." And they would say "No, no, no, that's
all water under the bridge. It's because

00:01:51.479 --> 00:01:53.320
they dig holes in our beaches."

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:56.479
This happened like six different times, and Sarah and I
would always comment upon it,

00:01:56.479 --> 00:02:00.329
apparently in the presence of Henry. And
then we went to a Dutch beach,

00:02:00.329 --> 00:02:04.290
and sure enough, there were all of these
people with, like, full sized shovels

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:08.520
digging, like, thirty foot deep holes, and
all of them were speaking German!

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:12.239
And Henry, somewhere in the deep recesses
of his one-year-old brain,

00:02:12.239 --> 00:02:15.300
remembered this, which makes me worry about
all the other things that he might

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:18.510
remember. Like the times during Liverpool games when I forget to use the

00:02:18.510 --> 00:02:21.300
word "darn" and also forget to use the word
"damn".

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:24.440
Anyway, Hank, I bring this up for two reasons.
First: I think it is time for German

00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:28.540
people and Dutch people to have a
constructive dialogue about the holes-

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:32.120
in-beaches phenomenon. And secondly: Henry
is three and a half years old and he

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:35.300
already has his first cultural
stereotype, hole-digging Germans.

00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:38.510
I think that says a lot about us as
humans. Anyway, I know there's nothing

00:02:38.510 --> 00:02:42.120
more boring than stories about other people's
kids, but whatever, they're adorable!

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:45.260
Okay, Hank, I just want to take a second to thank all of Nerdfighteria for being so understanding during

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:49.290
my paternity leave, and also to the
amazing guest hosts who made it so much fun.

00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:50.830
Frankly, more fun than, like,

00:02:50.830 --> 00:02:54.019
regular me. You can find links to all of their channels in the doobly-doo. Hank, thank you for this

00:02:54.019 --> 00:02:57.670
nice hotel room. I will see you when I go
downstairs after finishing editing this. Byeeee!

